WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.280
In November 2024, a PlayStation&nbsp;
user noticed something… odd.&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.320
Logged in, Astro Bot on the PS&nbsp;
Store was $44.99. But when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:16.000
logged out, it was $59.99?
They shared it on Reddit.&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.680
Weird, but maybe just some kind of discount?
A year later, another post.&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:28.480
Red Dead 2, same region, even the same&nbsp;
PlayStation, but two different prices?&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.800
More of these began to pop up.
What was happening?&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.560
Then, the site PSprices, which tracks PlayStation&nbsp;
Store prices, reported that Sony had been secretly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.800
experimenting with dynamic pricing.
Two users can secretly be given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.160
different discounts.
Is this even legal?&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.000
Unfortunately, this is just the beginning.
Sony is trying to squeeze customers for as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.440
much as possible, and something&nbsp;
far bigger is forcing their hand.&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.000
But it's not just Sony.
A gigantic battle over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:17.440
very nature of discounts is just beginning.
And some are doing much worse than Sony.&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:23.120
So, what was actually going on?
The team at PSPrices, the platform that tracks the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.000
PlayStation Store, noticed something strange.
Tension&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.840
The store's public-facing API&nbsp;
started including weird strings.&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:37.680
First was "IPT_OPR_TESTING". It was&nbsp;
attached to 17 games in 42 regions.&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:46.000
Then another: "IPT_PILOT". 139 games&nbsp;
across 68 regions, but in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:53.200
a lot of Sony's own titles.
Then another in March: "IPT_LTM".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.440
US only, 104 games.
What were these?&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.480
Users were secretly being assigned to control&nbsp;
and test groups. You might see a standard price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:08.800
or an experimental price, depending on the group.
So, the user who had a different price from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:14.320
wife? One would have been a control group and&nbsp;
the other a test group. Despite both being in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:20.400
same region and currency — even the same house.
"During the February PlayStation Store sales we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.400
found that experiment participants received&nbsp;
a different discount percentage than regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.640
users. For example, HELLDIVERS 2 was sold&nbsp;
to regular users at -25%, while experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:36.400
participants saw -56%."
But it gets even weirder.&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.120
IPT_LTM, for example — the US&nbsp;
one — was testing discounts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:47.920
against each other, with much bigger discounts.
The retail price for GTA5 was $39.99. Some users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.840
saw a discount to $29.99, others $26.99.
So what was going on?&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:01.120
People are calling it "Dynamic Pricing".
And it's "sort of, but not quite."&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.600
It's hard to say, but it doesn't seem like it&nbsp;
fluctuates enough based on real-time demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.440
to be called "Dynamic Pricing."
To be clear: no one was being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.800
charged above retail. Some users were&nbsp;
getting bigger discounts than others.&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.360
What's actually happening is that&nbsp;
Sony is testing price elasticity.&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.920
This is something businesses do to figure&nbsp;
out how sensitive you are to price changes.&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.400
For Sony, using A/B testing, they're trying&nbsp;
to figure out how much they need to lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:33.760
prices to get you to buy. Who will pay full&nbsp;
price, and who needs a bit more of a nudge.&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.080
This sort of pricing already exists.
Airlines do this all the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.840
which is why people frequently pay&nbsp;
different amounts for the same ticket.&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.560
Amazon and other big retailers&nbsp;
also do this, egregiously so.&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.960
And will send targeted coupons&nbsp;
to people based on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:56.000
shopping behavior or a "welcome back" discount.
Though very recently, this is getting a lot worse.&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:01.360
But for Sony, this feels different,&nbsp;
and unwarranted for video games.&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:06.400
There's a "social contract" with a&nbsp;
storefront. Everything is uniform.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:12.160
Everyone sees the same discount. That was&nbsp;
the promise, unlike with airlines or Amazon.&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.400
Hiding the discounts feels a bit weird,&nbsp;
plus Sony's strong monopoly position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:21.680
over the store basically means you pay&nbsp;
what they decide, or nothing at all.&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:26.800
And most of all: price elasticity&nbsp;
is also tied to supply.&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:33.920
Digital games don't run out. There's no supply.&nbsp;
No limited number of plane seats to fill.&nbsp;

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.320
Plus, what determines what group you're in?
Is it random, or something more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.200
"tailored," based on behavior?
Gamers are, naturally, pretty annoyed at this.&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.080
More and more posts began popping up.
Another user had Assassin's Creed Unity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.640
displaying a discounted price of £3.74&nbsp;
when browsed logged out, jumping to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:58.800
£9.99 once they logged into their account.
But moderators deleted that one, annoyingly.&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.880
The media quickly picked up on it.
Push Square reported that "We've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:08.080
able to replicate this on the browser-based PS&nbsp;
Store; when logged in, we see the higher price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.480
and it swaps to the lower one when&nbsp;
we're not, just as shown above."&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.960
"This is absolutely ridiculous&nbsp;
and extremely scummy."&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:22.160
"Shame on Sony. I'll be buying even&nbsp;
less digital games than I already do."&nbsp;

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:28.080
Someone shared the PSprices article on&nbsp;
Reddit, which got 1,100 comments in four days.&nbsp;

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.800
But why is this happening now?
As it turns out, Sony is caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:45.440
right in the middle of a huge&nbsp;
change, and they're being squeezed.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.600
