00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:41.120
To understand what's really going on… we need to&nbsp;
look no further than our old friend: big tech.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:46.560
AI companies — NVIDIA, Google, Microsoft&nbsp;
— are paying premium prices for high-speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:51.680
memory at a scale that consumer electronics&nbsp;
manufacturers simply can't compete with.&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:54.960
But they're not really&nbsp;
buying the same RAM as Sony;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:01.760
they're trying to get HBM: High Bandwidth Memory.
So, what does this have to do with PlayStation?&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:06.800
Well, consoles use a particular type of&nbsp;
DRAM: GDDR, and because companies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:12.320
making more HBM to keep up, they aren't&nbsp;
prioritizing other kinds of RAM. Micron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:17.040
the third-largest RAM supplier, has&nbsp;
left the consumer market entirely.&nbsp;

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:21.600
There's not enough to go around,&nbsp;
so prices have gone up a lot.&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:29.680
"DRAM contract prices have been revised upward,&nbsp;
from a previous estimate of 55–60% to now 90–95%."&nbsp;

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:34.000
So, Sony has a bit of a&nbsp;
problem: getting enough RAM.&nbsp;

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:39.040
In November 2025, Sony CFO Lin Tao told&nbsp;
investors that Sony had secured just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:45.120
enough memory supply to keep PS5 hardware&nbsp;
profitable, but only through March 2026.&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.840
Anything after that wasn't&nbsp;
guaranteed. And it gets worse.&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:56.560
PlayStation hardware margins are pretty thin.&nbsp;
Under 10%. Way more than before, but still low.&nbsp;

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:58.880
[Piers Harding-Rolls, Ampere Analysis]: "It&nbsp;
is likely that Sony had price protections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:04.800
for its components for a set period and this&nbsp;
may well have come to an end. With no sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:10.240
of prices easing ... Sony will have made the&nbsp;
move to protect its slim hardware margins."&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:16.400
To be clear: Sony as a whole isn't struggling.&nbsp;
Operating profits jumped 22% year-on-year in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:23.040
Q3 FY2025. PlayStation alone brought&nbsp;
in around $900 million, up 19%. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:27.760
things were getting worse, and fast.
With this road of uncertainty ahead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:32.320
it gives context to some decisions.
In Sony's Q3 earnings call, February&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:38.000
2026 — right at the end of Sony's RAM supply&nbsp;
— CFO Lin Tao said something interesting.&nbsp;

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:43.440
"We intend to minimize the impact of the increased&nbsp;
memory cost on this segment going forward by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:48.560
prioritizing monetization of the install base to&nbsp;
date and striving to further expand our software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:52.480
and network service revenue."
The installed base — aka:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:57.680
the people who already own a PS5.
So, what does all this have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.640
do with Sony's pricing experiment?
Sony takes a 30% cut from every PS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:08.240
Store purchase, like Apple and Steam,&nbsp;
even if it's not their own games.&nbsp;

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.560
Something which they're currently being sued for.
But now they're trying to get their "install&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:18.800
base" to buy more, by testing elasticity.
Console margins are slim and getting slimmer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:22.960
so why not extract more from the&nbsp;
area with far better margins?

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:28.320
Users are now expecting a price hike to PS Plus,&nbsp;
with some threatening to cancel if they do.&nbsp;

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:33.040
Others are suggesting buying a year's&nbsp;
subscription now before the price jumps.&nbsp;

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:36.720
Though… dunno how that sticks it to Sony?

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:41.120
On March 27th, 2026. PlayStation Blog.&nbsp;
Isabelle Tomatis, VP of Global Marketing.&nbsp;

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:46.080
"With continued pressures in the global&nbsp;
economic landscape, we've made the decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:52.105
to increase the prices of PS5, PS5 Pro, and&nbsp;
PlayStation Portal remote player globally."

00:10:52.105 --> 00:10:52.202
PS5 Disc Edition: $549.99 → $649.99. Up $100.
PS5 Digital Edition: $499.99 → $599.99. Up $100.&nbsp;

00:10:52.202 --> 00:10:54.320
PS5 Pro: $749.99 → $899.99. Up $150.
Since launch in November 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.320 --> 00:11:00.560
the PS5 Disc Edition has gone from&nbsp;
$499.99 to $649.99 — $150 more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:01.600
day one. (Lower third: With a&nbsp;
$50 increase in August 2025).&nbsp;

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:10.080
The PS5 Pro launched at $699.99 in late 2024.&nbsp;
It's now $899.99. $200 more in under two years.&nbsp;

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.480
I didn't realise the PlayStation was an&nbsp;
appreciating asset! I thought consoles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:17.840
were supposed to get cheaper, guys.
People are now expecting the Switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:22.240
2 to also see a price hike.
And that's not even mentioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:24.676
the new digital license controversy.
(Highlight: "now require an internet connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.676 --> 00:11:26.240
once every 30 days to validate the license")
The more things change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:30.960
the more they stay the same.
But something is changing.&nbsp;

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:35.040
This type of tailored, opaque pricing&nbsp;
from Sony is becoming more common.&nbsp;

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:48.320
There's a huge battle waging right now over&nbsp;
the very nature of sales and discounts.&nbsp;

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:53.120
More and more companies are trying to&nbsp;
change the way discounts work altogether.&nbsp;

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:59.120
Even worse than Sony, many companies will&nbsp;
secretly charge you more. And not at random.&nbsp;

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:02.960
Though I'm not talking about&nbsp;
dynamic pricing or surge pricing.&nbsp;

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:08.880
Where prices change based on general demand, time&nbsp;
of day, scarcity — why hotels cost more on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:15.200
same day as a big sporting event. That's normal.
I'm talking about surveillance pricing.

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:17.760
Instacart began using AI price experiments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:23.360
and a non-profit found that prices&nbsp;
differed by up to 23% per item.&nbsp;

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:27.600
Target's app will display higher&nbsp;
prices on the same products — like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:32.800
$148 more for the same vacuum cleaner.
Even weirder, these price changes came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:37.520
when you were inside the store, versus&nbsp;
on the far side of the parking lot.&nbsp;

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:42.640
Staples will charge you more if you have fewer&nbsp;
options — like if you're near a competitor.&nbsp;

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:45.840
Delta announced it would start&nbsp;
deploying surveillance pricing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:51.680
though it quickly backpedalled after backlash.
Hotel prices in Manhattan jumped by as much as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:58.640
$511 per night when browsing from a Bay Area&nbsp;
IP address compared to Phoenix or Kansas City.&nbsp;

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:05.280
Don't even get me started on Amazon.
Though this pricing has started a war.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:09.280
This year, lawmakers across the US&nbsp;
have introduced more than 70 bills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:14.880
targeting surveillance pricing — travel,&nbsp;
grocery, rental markets, and now gaming.&nbsp;

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:18.640
On March 5th, 2026, the House&nbsp;
Oversight Committee formally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:23.440
launched an investigation into it — sending&nbsp;
letters to major platform companies demanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:27.520
documentation on their pricing algorithms.
[Chairman Comer]: "Surveillance pricing can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:32.480
difficult to detect because consumers rarely&nbsp;
have a view into what information a company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:37.360
has about them, what the prices they see are&nbsp;
based on, or what prices other customers may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:42.880
be seeing for the same goods or services.
Often this takes place in a 'black box'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:47.600
environment where consumers do not know&nbsp;
that personalized pricing is taking place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:51.600
or what information collected&nbsp;
about them is driving prices."&nbsp;

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:56.160
Maryland just passed the Protection From&nbsp;
Predatory Pricing Act — the first law of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:01.040
kind in the US — banning retailers and delivery&nbsp;
services from using personal data to tailor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:07.920
prices. Though it might not be enough.
Consumer Reports said it had "too many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:11.520
industry-friendly loopholes, and&nbsp;
weak enforcement provisions."&nbsp;

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:16.640
But Maryland is just the beginning.
Hawaii, California, Illinois,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:20.720
Kentucky, Connecticut — more than&nbsp;
a dozen states have bills pending.

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:25.600
Sony's pricing might not be as bad as some&nbsp;
of these other cases, based on your income,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:30.560
location, or other private factors.
But it's along the same train of thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:34.240
and experiments are often the&nbsp;
first step of a bigger plan.&nbsp;

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:38.960
The worst part of all these pricing&nbsp;
strategies is the lack of transparency.&nbsp;

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:44.400
In 1970, economist George Akerlof published&nbsp;
the paper "Market for Lemons," which won&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:48.400
him a Nobel Prize and changed the&nbsp;
way we understood the free market.&nbsp;

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:56.000
In the used car market, sellers know whether their&nbsp;
car is good or a "lemon," but buyers can't tell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:02.800
So buyers just pay the "average price," instead&nbsp;
of more for the good car and less for the bad.&nbsp;

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:06.320
With more information, customers&nbsp;
make better purchasing decisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:08.400
and better quality products win.&nbsp;

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:13.680
But in a "black box," customers lose&nbsp;
and spend more for worse products.

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:17.360
Currently, Sony is in hot water&nbsp;
over another problem as well.&nbsp;

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:23.280
They make so much money from the PS Store because&nbsp;
they charge 30%, since they hold all the power.&nbsp;

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:28.400
But now they're caught in a multi-billion&nbsp;
dollar lawsuit, as some think this is an illegal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:39.533
monopoly that has caused prices to be too high.
